&gt; &gt; application_traffic_secret_0 ... application_traffic_secret_N ...<=
br>
&gt; application_traffic_secret_N&#43;1<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; It might be better to use a single `application_traffic_secret`<br>
&gt; instead of multiple `application_traffic_secret_XXX`:<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; ```<br>
&gt; application_traffic_secret =3D<br>
&gt; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; HKDF-Expand-Label(application_traffic_secre=
t,<br>
&gt; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &=
nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &quot;traffic upd&quot;, &quot;&quot;, Hash.length)<br>
&gt; ```<br>
<br>
In my opinion this would be less clear.<br>
<br>
<br>
&gt; ## Presentation Language<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; Formally, it is necessary to explain `a^b`.<br>
<br>
I think this is well-enough known that it's not necessary. It<br>
hasn't been an issue in the past AFAICT.<br>
<br>
<br>
&gt; Recommendation: define `&#43;=3D` for enums:<br>
&gt; ```<br>
&gt; NamedGroup &#43;=3D group_a(0xFEFF);<br>
&gt; ...<br>
&gt; NamedGroup &#43;=3D { group_b(0xFEFE), group_c(0xFEFD) };<br>
&gt; ```<br>
&gt; This facilitates extending enums in external specifications.<br>
<br>
We usually just do this verbally. I'm opposed to adding new<br>
syntax at this time.<br>
<br>
<br>
&gt; # Questions<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; ## 4.1.2<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; &gt; The client will also send a ClientHello when the server has<br>
&gt; responded to its ClientHello with a HelloRetryRequest. In that case,<br=
>
&gt; the client MUST send the same ClientHello without modification, except<=
br>
&gt; as follows: ...<br>
&gt; * Optionally adding, removing, or changing the length of the &quot;padd=
ing&quot;<br>
&gt; &nbsp; extension [RFC7685].<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; Q: Allowing the &quot;padding&quot; extension to be modified makes it<b=
r>
&gt; problematic to reconstruct the first ClientHello from the second, does<=
br>
&gt; not it? (The reconstruction is mentioned in<br>
&gt; [<a href=3D"https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/7rLQEHrqKaYo7u31i=
NBNsPI_OVg/">https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/7rLQEHrqKaYo7u31iNBNs=
PI_OVg/</a>]<br>
&gt; but not in the specification).<br>
<br>
As you say it's not in the specification, so it's the job of the<br>
receiver to figure out what to do, potentially by memoizing<br>
the extension in the cookie, or just memoizing the whole hash<br>
state.<br>
<br>
<br>
&gt; ## 4.6.2<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; &gt; In addition, clients which receive multiple CertificateRequests in=
<br>
&gt; close succession MAY respond to them in a different order than they<br>
&gt; were received (the certificate_request_context value allows the server<=
br>
&gt; to disambiguate the responses).<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; Q: Can messages from different responses to CertificateRequests be<br>
&gt; interleaved? Is it possible, for example, to obtain the following<br>
&gt; transcript:<br>
&gt; ```<br>
&gt; CR_1, CR_2, CT_1, CT_2, CV_2, CV_1, F_1, F_2<br>
&gt; ```<br>
&gt; ? If so, how does the server differentiate between CV_1 and CV_2 or<br>
&gt; between F_2 and F_1 considering that these messages don't contain<br>
&gt; `certificate_request_context`?<br>
<br>
No. This is forbidden earlier.<br>
&nbsp; <br>
&nbsp; &nbsp;All of the client's messages for a given response MUST appear<b=
r>
&nbsp; &nbsp;consecutively on the wire with no intervening messages of other=
<br>
&nbsp; &nbsp;types.<br>
<br>
I sharpened this slightly in the current text by adding:<br>
<br>
&nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;or from other responses.&quot;<br>
<br>
<br>
-Ekr<br>
<br>
<br>
<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
</div>
<br>
<div class=3D"gmail_quote gmail_quote_container">
<div dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"gmail_attr">On Thu, Jul 24, 2025 at 5:32=E2=80=AFA=
M Sergey Agievich &lt;Agievich=3D<a href=3D"mailto:40bsu.by@dmarc.ietf.org">=
40bsu.by@dmarc.ietf.org</a>&gt; wrote:<br>
</div>
<blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-l=
eft:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex">
<div class=3D"msg3460240557453431598">
<div dir=3D"ltr">
<div id=3D"m_3460240557453431598divtagdefaultwrapper" style=3D"font-size:12p=
t;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif" dir=3D"ltr">
<p></p>
<div></div>
<div>Hi,&nbsp;</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>I have enough carefully read draft-ietf-tls-rfc8446bis and prepared</di=
v>
<div>some comments. I hope they will help improve the editorial quality of</=
div>
<div>the specification.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>I also have 2 technical questions. It would be great to have answers.</=
div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Best,</div>
<div>Sergey</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div># Comments</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.1.3</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; If a TLS 1.3 ClientHello is received with any other value in this<=
/div>
<div>field, the server MUST abort the handshake with an &quot;illegal_parame=
ter&quot;</div>
<div>alert.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>`ClientHello` -&gt; `ServerHello`, server -&gt; client.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; Upon receipt of a HelloRetryRequest, the client MUST ... process t=
he</div>
<div>extensions .... Otherwise, the client MUST process all extensions in</d=
iv>
<div>the HelloRetryRequest...</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Twice about the same thing (processing the extensions).</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.1.4</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; As with the ServerHello, a HelloRetryRequest MUST NOT contain any<=
/div>
<div>extensions that were not first offered by the client.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>It appears to be the first explanation that the ServerHello /</div>
<div>HelloRetryRequest extensions must match the ClientHello ones.&nbsp;</di=
v>
<div>So &quot;As with the ServerHello&quot; does not work.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.2.1</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; A server which negotiates a version of TLS prior to TLS 1.3 MUST s=
et</div>
<div>ServerHello.version and MUST NOT send the &quot;supported_versions&quot=
;</div>
<div>extension.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>`ServerHello.version` -&gt; `ServerHello.legacy_version`.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.2.8</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; Each KeyShareEntry value MUST correspond to a group offered in the=
</div>
<div>&quot;supported_groups&quot; extension and MUST appear in the same orde=
r. [A]</div>
<div>.</div>
<div><span style=3D"font-size:12pt">&gt; If using (EC)DHE key establishment,=
 servers offer exactly one</span></div>
<div>KeyShareEntry in the ServerHello. This value MUST be in the same group<=
/div>
<div>as the KeyShareEntry value offered by the client that the server has</d=
iv>
<div>selected for the negotiated key exchange. [B]</div>
<div>.</div>
<div><span style=3D"font-size:12pt">&gt; Servers MUST NOT send a KeyShareEnt=
ry for any group not indicated in</span></div>
<div>the client's &quot;supported_groups&quot;. [C]</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>[C] is redundant: it automatically holds if [A] and [B] hold.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.2.11.3</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; Clients are permitted to &quot;stream&quot; 0-RTT data until they =
receive the</div>
<div>server's Finished, only then sending the EndOfEarlyData message,</div>
<div>followed by the rest of the handshake. In order to avoid deadlocks,</di=
v>
<div>when accepting &quot;early_data&quot;, servers MUST process the client'=
s</div>
<div>ClientHello and then immediately send their flight of messages, rather<=
/div>
<div>than waiting for the client's EndOfEarlyData message before sending</di=
v>
<div>its ServerHello.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>A better place for this paragraph seems to be 4.2.10&nbsp;<span style=3D=
"font-size:12pt">(early_data) or&nbsp;</span></div>
<div><span style=3D"font-size:12pt">4.5 (EndOfEarlyData).</span></div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.6.1</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; The value of zero indicates that the ticket should be discarded</d=
iv>
<div>immediately.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&quot;discarded immediately&quot; -&gt; &quot;discarded immediately aft=
er use&quot;.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; If such an indication is provided (externally or by any other</div=
>
<div>means), clients MAY resume with a different SNI value.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>It is difficult to understand that &quot;indication&quot; refers (indee=
d?) to</div>
<div>the beginning of the paragraph.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 6.1</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; close_notify: ... Any data received after a closure alert has been=
</div>
<div>received MUST be ignored.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; Any data received after a close_notify alert has been received MUS=
T</div>
<div>be ignored.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Duplication.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 7.2</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; Once the handshake is complete, it is possible for either side to<=
/div>
<div>update its sending traffic keys.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>keys -&gt; key.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; application_traffic_secret_0 ... application_traffic_secret_N ...<=
/div>
<div>application_traffic_secret_N&#43;1</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>It might be better to use a single `application_traffic_secret`&nbsp;</=
div>
<div>instead of multiple `application_traffic_secret_XXX`:&nbsp;</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>```</div>
<div>application_traffic_secret =3D</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; HKDF-Expand-Label(application_traffic_secre=
t,</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &=
nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &quot;traffic upd&quot;, &quot;&quot;, Hash.length)</div=
>
<div>```</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## Presentation Language</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Formally, it is necessary to explain `a^b`.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Recommendation: define `&#43;=3D` for enums:</div>
<div>```</div>
<div>NamedGroup &#43;=3D group_a(0xFEFF);</div>
<div>...</div>
<div>NamedGroup &#43;=3D { group_b(0xFEFE), group_c(0xFEFD) };</div>
<div>```</div>
<div>This facilitates extending enums in external specifications.</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div># Questions</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.1.2</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; The client will also send a ClientHello when the server has</div>
<div>responded to its ClientHello with a HelloRetryRequest. In that case,</d=
iv>
<div>the client MUST send the same ClientHello without modification, except<=
/div>
<div>as follows: ...</div>
<div>* Optionally adding, removing, or changing the length of the &quot;padd=
ing&quot;</div>
<div>&nbsp; extension [RFC7685].</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Q: Allowing the &quot;padding&quot; extension to be modified makes it</=
div>
<div>problematic to reconstruct the first ClientHello from the second, does<=
/div>
<div>not it? (The reconstruction is mentioned in</div>
<div>[<a href=3D"https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/7rLQEHrqKaYo7u31i=
NBNsPI_OVg/" target=3D"_blank">https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/7rL=
QEHrqKaYo7u31iNBNsPI_OVg/</a>]&nbsp;</div>
<div>but not in the specification).</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>## 4.6.2</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&gt; In addition, clients which receive multiple CertificateRequests in=
</div>
<div>close succession MAY respond to them in a different order than they</di=
v>
<div>were received (the certificate_request_context value allows the server<=
/div>
<div>to disambiguate the responses).</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Q: Can messages from different responses to CertificateRequests be&nbsp=
;</div>
<div>interleaved? Is it possible, for example, to obtain the following&nbsp;=
</div>
<div>transcript:&nbsp;</div>
<div>```</div>
<div>CR_1, CR_2, CT_1, CT_2, CV_2, CV_1, F_1, F_2</div>
<div>```</div>
<div>? If so, how does the server differentiate between CV_1 and CV_2 or</di=
v>
<div>between F_2 and F_1 considering that these messages don't contain</div>
<div>`certificate_request_context`?</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<br>
<p></p>
</div>
